\subsubsection{Adaptation to shifted front ends}

\IfFileExists{tables/frontend_adaptation.tex}{%
Table~\ref{tab:frontend_adaptation} reports the complete frozen \(8\times4\) adaptation matrix. Supplementary Fig.~\ref{S-fig:frontend_adaptation} visualizes the corresponding relative recovery percentages. Results retain negative cells and expose the trade-off between recovery on a target front end and retention on native SAM3D inputs.
The frozen reference checkpoint remains best on its native SAM3D input (0.1551 MPJPE), but its error rises to 0.4385--0.5389 on the three alternative front ends. Full target-specific adaptation reduces the matched-front-end error to 0.2290 for VideoPose3D, 0.2741 for PoseFormer, and 0.2393 for MotionBERT, corresponding to recoveries of 57.5\%, 48.9\%, and 45.4\% relative to the reference checkpoint evaluated on the same front end. Mixed full adaptation provides the most balanced alternative-front-end results (0.2363, 0.2751, and 0.2464), but increases SAM3D error to 0.1809, a 16.7\% retention penalty. Thus, adaptation substantially mitigates front-end shift, while no adapted model dominates the frozen reference checkpoint on every input source.
\input{tables/frontend_adaptation.tex}

}{%
\paragraph{Pending result} Front-end adaptation results will be inserted only after all seven training runs and all 32 test cells pass the no-leakage and full-split validators.
}

\FloatBarrier
\subsection{Scaling beyond two views}

\IfFileExists{tables/nview_scaling.tex}{%
Figure~\ref{fig:nview_scaling} shows accuracy and bootstrap intervals under frozen pairwise composition, while Table~\ref{tab:nview_efficiency} reports its computational cost. Supplementary Table~\ref{S-tab:nview_scaling} provides the numerical means, standard deviations, and intervals for all methods. Accepted and rejected camera groups are retained in the runtime manifest so that missing common frames are not silently converted into favorable subsets.
\input{tables/nview_efficiency.tex}
The two-view configuration captures the largest gain: it reduces MPJPE from the single-view value of 0.1715 to 0.1295 (24.5\%) with one pair forward and 2.02~ms serial fusion latency. Three views reduce MPJPE by a further 9.1\% relative to two views, but require three pair forwards and 6.02~ms (2.98-fold) latency. Four views reach 0.1158 MPJPE, a 10.6\% reduction relative to two views, while requiring six pair forwards and 12.06~ms (5.96-fold) latency; the incremental gain from three to four views is only 1.6\%. Thus, additional views remain useful when accuracy is prioritized, whereas two views provide the most favorable accuracy--cost operating point for the main CanonFuse3D configuration.
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{figures/extension/nview_scaling.pdf}
\caption{N-view scaling under frozen pairwise CanonFuse3D composition. Error bars show paired-group bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals. The oracle is an upper bound.}
\label{fig:nview_scaling}
\end{figure}
}{%
\paragraph{Pending result} The N-view table and figure will be inserted only after the strict common-frame filter and the complete N=1--4 result matrix pass the artifact validator.
}

\FloatBarrier
\subsection{Automatic synchronization diagnostic}

\IfFileExists{tables/temporal_alignment.tex}{%
Figure~\ref{fig:temporal_alignment} compares uncorrected, automatically corrected, and oracle streams across the eleven injected offsets. Supplementary Table~\ref{S-tab:temporal_alignment} reports the exact errors together with lag-estimation accuracy and correction-activation rates. The confidence threshold (0.09296) was fixed from validation data before these test results were read. The result is directionally asymmetric. Automatic correction improves MPJPE for every tested positive lag: at \(+5\) frames, it reduces error from 0.1537 to 0.1506, versus the 0.1488 oracle. It also gives smaller improvements at \(\pm0.5\) frames. In contrast, it worsens every tested negative integer lead; at \(-5\) frames, error increases from 0.1484 to 0.1498. At zero offset, 41.5\% of samples activate a nonzero correction and MPJPE increases slightly from 0.14879 to 0.14884. Exact lag accuracy is only 13.2\% at \(+5\) frames. Thus, the descriptor is a useful diagnostic and partially recovers positive delays, but it is not a reliable bidirectional synchronization solution. We do not use it to strengthen the main CanonFuse3D comparison.
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{figures/extension/temporal_alignment.pdf}
\caption{MPJPE under injected right-stream offsets on the fixed central 20-frame interval. Oracle restores the original unperturbed right stream; automatic correction uses the validation-calibrated confidence threshold.}
\label{fig:temporal_alignment}
\end{figure}
}{%
\paragraph{Pending result} Temporal-alignment results will be inserted only after all 33 offset-condition cells and the validation-threshold provenance pass validation.
}
